# D21 prover notes: why a goal after an `Unknown` can read `Valid`

Measured on this host: why3 1.8.2, z3 5.1.0 (`z3 --version`).

## Why3 does not recognise Z3 5.1.0 (and uses the fallback driver)
Prover Z3 version 5.1.0 is not recognized.
  Known versions for this prover: ^4\.8\.[7-9]$, ^4\.8\.1[0-9]+$,
  ^4\.9\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.10\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.11\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.12\.[0-9]+$,
  ^4\.13\.[0-9]+$.
Prover Z3 (alternative: noBV) version 5.1.0 is not recognized.
  Known versions for this prover: 4.7.1, 4.8.0, 4.8.1, 4.8.3, 4.8.4, 4.8.5,
  4.8.6, ^4\.8\.[7-9]$, ^4\.8\.1[0-9]+$, ^4\.9\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.10\.[0-9]+$,
  ^4\.11\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.12\.[0-9]+$, ^4\.13\.[0-9]+$.

## A false `lemma` is an assumption for later goals in the same module
module LemmaThenGoal
  use int.Int

  (* A false lemma. Why3 treats earlier `lemma` declarations as hypotheses for
     later goals in the same module, whether or not their own proof succeeded. *)
  lemma false_first:
    forall x: int. x = x + 1

  goal false_second:
    1 = 2
end
--- why3 prove -P z3 lemma_then_goal.mlw
File "lemma_then_goal.mlw", line 7, characters 4-28:
Goal false_first.
Prover result is: Unknown (sat) (0.00s, 19 steps).

File "lemma_then_goal.mlw", line 10, characters 4-9:
Goal false_second.
Prover result is: Valid (0.01s, 15 steps).

--- the SMT task Why3 sends for the second goal
;;; generated by SMT-LIB2 driver
;;; SMT-LIB2 driver: bit-vectors, common part
;;; generated by SMT-LIB strings
;;; SMT-LIB2: integer arithmetic
;; "false_first"
(assert (forall ((x Int)) (= x (+ x 1))))

;; Goal "false_second"
;; File "lemma_then_goal.mlw", line 9, characters 7-19
(assert
  (not (= 1 2)))

(check-sat)

## The same two statements as `goal`s: no shared hypothesis
module GoalThenGoal
  use int.Int

  (* The same two statements as `goal`s: goals are not shared as hypotheses. *)
  goal false_first:
    forall x: int. x = x + 1

  goal false_second:
    1 = 2
end
--- why3 prove -P z3 goal_then_goal.mlw
File "goal_then_goal.mlw", line 6, characters 4-28:
Goal false_first.
Prover result is: Unknown (sat) (0.00s, 19 steps).

File "goal_then_goal.mlw", line 9, characters 4-9:
Goal false_second.
Prover result is: Unknown (sat) (0.00s, 11 steps).

--- the SMT task for the second goal
;;; generated by SMT-LIB2 driver
;;; SMT-LIB2 driver: bit-vectors, common part
;;; generated by SMT-LIB strings
;;; SMT-LIB2: integer arithmetic
;; Goal "false_second"
;; File "goal_then_goal.mlw", line 8, characters 7-19
(assert
  (not (= 1 2)))

(check-sat)

## Conclusion
The second goal is `Valid` in the first file because the contradictory
lemma is asserted in its SMT task, not because of the prover version or
an `Unknown` answer to the previous goal. Why3 would report the same on
any recognised Z3. No prover version has to be pinned away: every D21
gate requires *every* goal `Valid`, and the audit proves each mutation
witness as the only goal in its file so no earlier declaration can be
inherited. Z3 5.1.0 being unrecognised is a separate, real caveat:
`why3 config detect` binds it to the generic driver, which is written for
Z3 <= 4.3.1. The gates pin `-P z3` and the measured versions above.
